TIP:  For the second set of (A)’s in the intro, there’s a second guitar picking out notes within the strings formed by the (A), except for a rogue <f#> (<g> when capo’d) on the 4th string.  So to emulate this, play this sequence by focusing your strum on the treble strings, then middle, then bass, and then middle again.  This same pattern is depicted by the double (A) (A) after “Why not me?”  You’ll hear it when you get to it.
 
TIP:  This is a bit of an advanced technique (and you can ignore it and the song will sound just fine) but there’s a high treble ping of an <a> note at the 1st string/5th fret which is an octave above one that lives on the 3rd string/2nd fret present when playing a usual open (A).  Try playing this (A) chord as shown in the diagram.  You can either form the chord as a barre, like you would an (F#m) shape, and then using your pinky to stretch over to cover the 1st string/5th fret.  Or, play the regular (A) with a concave arch of the index finger, covering strings 2 - 3 - 4 all with the pad of your index finger but lifting up to avoid the 1st string.  Then collapse down to cover strings 1 - 2 - 3 - 4 and use your pinky to cover the 1st string/5th fret.   


